          \  \n  – Echologics managed acoustic districts: US $0.80-$1.20 per foot/yr. Typical 100-mile DMA ≈US $80-120k. \
          \ \n  ➔ Range is realistic.\n\nClaim 5 – “AWWA estimates $1 T infrastructure investment needed over 25 yrs”  \n\
          • AWWA “Buried No Longer” (2011) projected US $1.0-$1.7 T through 2040; reiterated 2024.  \n  ➔ Supported.\n\n────────────────────────────────────────\n\
          7. INSTALLED BASE SNAPSHOT\n────────────────────────────────────────\nNorth America\n• “40 %+ of utilities have\
          \ adopted IoT/advanced monitoring” (BusinessResearchInsights, 2024).  \n• Smart home market: >30 % of new U.S. homes\
          \ already include a leak sensor; forecast 50 % by 2027 (TechSciResearch, 2024).  \n• City-wide deployments:  \n\
          \  – Toronto: 550 pressure/Acoustic sensors (Echologics, 2023)  \n  – San Antonio SAWS: 250k AMI meters with leak\
          \ analytics layer (Badger, 2024)\n\nEuropean Union\n• UK: >500,000 acoustic loggers via Thames, Severn, Anglian\
          \ (Ofwat PR24 submissions, 2024).  \n• France: SUEZ ON’Connect >1.5 M IoT meters (SUEZ press kit, 2024).  \n• Spain:\
          \ Canal de Isabel II – 7,400 district meters feeding TaKaDu since 2022.\n\n────────────────────────────────────────\n\
          SIGNIFICANT VARIANCES & DATA GAPS\n────────────────────────────────────────\n• TAM figures vary depending on whether\
          \ oil & gas is included (4-5 % CAGR) or only water (11-15 % CAGR).  \n• Leak-detection share of SWM (22-28 %) is\
          \ mostly derived from vendor revenue splits; few analyst reports publish it explicitly.  \n• Latest primary research\
          \ on installed base is sparse; most numbers come from utility case studies or vendor presentations, not syndicated\
          \ market reports.\n\n────────────────────────────────────────\nBOTTOM LINE\nThe IoT-enabled, AI-based leak-detection\
          \ opportunity in water utilities is a US $5-7 B global niche today, growing low-to-mid-teens annually. North America\
          \ represents roughly US $2 B of near-term SAM, Europe ~US $1.6 B, with regulatory pressure, aging infrastructure\
          \ and workforce constraints acting as strong catalysts. Market is still fragmented, allowing new entrants room to\
          \ capture share with differentiated analytics, SaaS delivery, or sensor innovations."
