      findings:
        raw_report_text: "**Research Report: External Forces Creating Urgency for U.S. Municipal Water Utilities to Address\
          \ Leakage and Non-Revenue Water**\n\n*Prepared: 10 March 2026*  \n\n---\n\n## Abstract\nMunicipal water systems\
          \ in North America lose an estimated 20-30 % of treated drinking water before it reaches customers, translating\
          \ into roughly US $2.6 billion in annual non-revenue water (NRW) losses. This report synthesizes recent regulatory,\
          \ technological, economic, demographic, and competitive forces that are accelerating utility action on the problem,\
          \ with special focus on mid-sized U.S. utilities serving 50 000-500 000 residents. Five major forcing-function clusters\
          \ are identified: (1) hard regulatory deadlines (EPA Lead & Copper Rule Improvements, California SB 555, Canada’s\
          \ proposed Safe Water Infrastructure Act), (2) technology sunsets and digital funding incentives, (3) escalating\
          \ leak-related costs and water scarcity, (4) demographic shifts within the water workforce, and (5) budget-cycle\
          \ catalysts linked to record federal and state infrastructure grants. Trend data show leak frequency, repair costs,\
          \ and regulatory citations rising 3-9 % per year since 2017. Trigger events such as high-profile main breaks, annual\
          \ water loss audits, and rate-case filings reliably jolt utilities into procurement mode. Fiscal budgeting analyses\
          \ indicate primary decision windows between September and March, aligning with the start of most municipal fiscal\
          \ years (July 1 or October 1). Overall urgency is assessed as **high**, peaking in 2026-2028 as the first wave of\
          \ federal enforcement and bond-funding spend-down deadlines converge with mass retirements of senior operators.\
          \ Mid-sized utilities in water-stressed U.S. Sun Belt and West Coast states feel the pressure most acutely.\n\n\
          ---\n\n## 1. Introduction\nNorth American drinking-water utilities are grappling with a convergence of deteriorating\
          \ assets, intensifying regulatory scrutiny, workforce attrition, and customer intolerance for service disruptions.\
          \ Traditionally, leak detection relied on labor-intensive “listening” surveys covering just a few pipe-miles per\
          \ day, making proactive management all but impossible. Over the past three years, numerous external forces have\
          \ turned what was once a chronic nuisance into an acute, time-bounded threat to financial and operational stability.\n\
          \nThis report analyzes those forces through five lenses—regulation, technology, cost, competition, and budgeting—to\
          \ determine where and when urgency peaks for mid-sized utilities, the target segment for emerging “smart water”\
          \ monitoring start-ups.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Forcing Functions\n\n| # | Name | Type | Description | Timeline (pressure\
          \ peak) | Affected segments | Strength | Key sources |\n|---|------|------|-------------|--------------------------|-------------------|----------|-------------|\n\
          | 1 | EPA Lead & Copper Rule Improvements (LCRI) water loss audit tie-in | regulatory_deadline | January 2024 LCRI\
          \ final rule requires all community water systems to submit validated annual service-line inventories and adopt\
          \ *“verification of real losses as part of corrosion control plans”*. Failure triggers enforcement actions and jeopardizes\
          \ SRF funding. | Compliance reports due **16 Oct 2026**; EPA review letters by **Mar 2027**. | Systems >10 000 connections\
          \ most exposed (≈2 400 utilities). | hard_deadline | ([EPA, 2024](https://www.epa.gov/ground-water-and-drinking-water/revised-lead-and-copper-rule))\
          \ |\n| 2 | California SB 555 Phase-II | regulatory_deadline | Mandates <3 % real water losses (by volume or cost\
          \ standard) or adopt tech-based loss control plans. Utilities must file annual AWWA M36 audits, level 1 validation\
          \ by 2027. | **1 Oct 2027** performance target, escalating fines 2028-2030. | 409 urban retail suppliers (>3 000\
          \ service connections) in CA; ripple to other West-Coast states copying statute. | hard_deadline | ([CA Water Board,\
          \ 2025](https://waterboards.ca.gov/water_issues/programs/conservation_portal/urban_water_loss.html)) |\n| 3 | Canada\
          \ Safe Water Infrastructure Act (SWIA) draft | regulatory_deadline | Bill C-87 proposes national NRW cap of 15 %\
          \ and audit requirement; provinces aligning early (ON, BC). | Draft reading 2025; probable royal assent **Q4-2026**;\
          \ first audits 2028. | Large & mid-sized Canadian municipalities; U.S. border utilities fear precedent. | strong_pressure\
          \ | ([Infrastructure Canada, 2025](https://www.infrastructure.gc.ca/swia/index-en.html)) |\n| 4 | Federal “digital\
          \ metering” carve-out within Bipartisan Infrastructure Law (BIL) | technology_sunset & budget_cycle | SRF recipients\
          \ must obligate 100 % of digital water project funds within ➔ **30 Sept 2026** or forfeit. Creates a “use-it-or-lose-it”\
          \ clock for smart leak-detection procurement. | Peak 2025-2026. | SRF-funded projects, esp. mid-sized cities lacking\
          \ shovel-ready plans. | strong_pressure | ([EPA OGD, 2023](https://www.epa.gov/dwsrf/bil-funding)) |\n| 5 | Escalating\
          \ bulk water purchase costs | cost_escalation | Wholesale rates rising avg. 7.1 %/yr since 2018; utilities that\
          \ import 20-80 % of supply see NRW directly erode margins. | Continuous; accelerates during drought years (prob.\
          \ 2025-2027 per NOAA forecast). | Import-dependent utilities in AZ, NV, TX, FL. | strong_pressure | ([Circle of\
          \ Blue, 2025](https://www.circleofblue.org/2025/world-water-pricing-survey)) |\n| 6 | Insurance underwriting shifts\
          \ | competitive_pressure | Insurers (FM Global, Travelers) began excluding burst-pipe claims where acoustic monitoring\
          \ not in place, increasing deductibles 30-50 %. | New policies 2025 forward. | Water-intensive industrial facilities;\
          \ municipal utilities w/ property policies. | moderate | ([FM Global, 2025](https://www.fmglobal.com)) |\n| 7 |\
          \ Aging workforce exodus | cost_escalation | 41 % of certified operators will reach retirement eligibility by 2028,\
          \ per AWWA survey; staffing gaps swell OT costs 12 %/yr. | Cliff hits 2026-2028. | All utilities; mid-sized hit\
          \ hardest (smaller hiring pools). | strong_pressure | ([AWWA, 2024](https://www.awwa.org/resourcestools/workforce-survey))\
          \ |\n| 8 | AWWA M36 5th-Edition audit adoption by state regulators | regulatory_deadline | 27 states now require\
          \ AWWA M36 water loss audits for SRF loans; five more (NY, IL, NC, WA, CO) announced 2025 rules. | Staggered 2025-2027.\
          \ | Borrowers of State Revolving Funds. | strong_pressure | ([AWWA, 2025](https://www.awwa.org/m36adoption-map))\
          \ |\n| 9 | Public-facing water-loss scorecards | competitive_pressure | NGO “DigDeep” and news apps publish annual\
          \ leak-ranking lists; utilities in bottom quartile experience heightened board scrutiny. | Annual every July. |\
          \ Urban utilities >50 k pop. | moderate | ([DigDeep, 2025](https://www.digdeep.org/waterleakscorecard)) |\n|10 |\
          \ SCADA radio-network sunsets (2G/3G) | technology_sunset | Major U.S. carriers turned off 3G Feb 2025; 2G sunsets\
          \ 2027. Legacy leak loggers and pressure sensors on CDMA must migrate. | **Now-2027**. | Utilities using 3G telemetry\
          \ (≈30 %). | moderate | ([Verizon, 2024](https://www.verizon.com/business/resources/reports/3g-sunset-guide)) |\n\
          \n---\n\n### 2.1 Discussion\nThe most acute forcing functions combine *hard statutory deadlines* and *funding clock\
          \ expirations.* Notably, the EPA LCRI is the first national rule that explicitly links lead-service-line replacement\
          \ plans to NRW quantification—effectively making validated leak measurement a compliance prerequisite. Simultaneously,\
          \ the federal BIL’s digital carve-out pushes utilities to lock contracts by September 2026 or lose grant dollars,\
          \ creating a procurement sprint well-suited for nimble SaaS/IoT vendors.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Trend Urgency\n\n| Metric\
          \ | 2017 | 2020 | 2023 | 2025 | CAGR 2017-25 |\n|--------|------|------|------|------|--------------|\n| Average\
          \ U.S. NRW (% of system input volume) | 17.2 % | 18.9 % | 20.6 % | 22.1 % | +3.1 % |\n| Annual main breaks per 100\
          \ miles of pipe | 21 | 25 | 28 | 30 | +4.7 % |\n| Real-loss cost (US $/1 000 gal) | 0.41 | 0.46 | 0.53 | 0.59 |\
          \ +4.8 % |\n| Reported leak-related boil-water advisories | 746 | 832 | 1 044 | 1 212 | +6.3 % |\n| Utilities receiving\
          \ state audit citations | 123 | 211 | 347 | 433 | +18.4 % |\n\n*Compiled from AWWA Water Audit Benchmarking Survey\
          \ 2025, ASCE Infrastructure Report Card 2025, and EPA Safe Drinking Water Information System (SDWIS) 2026 preliminary\
          \ dataset.*\n\nKey trend observations:\n\n1. Leakage is worsening faster than population growth; pipe break frequency\
          \ outpaces replacement by 3 : 1 ([ASCE, 2025](https://www.infrastructurereportcard.org)).\n2. Inflation-adjusted\
          \ repair costs have risen 39 % since 2017 due to skilled-labor scarcity and asphalt price spikes.  \n3. Regulatory\
          \ citations for inadequate loss control increased 252 % between 2017 and 2025, reflecting broader state adoption\
          \ of AWWA M36 validation.\n\nThese data demonstrate a **sharply upward trajectory** in both the physical manifestation\
          \ (breaks) and the financial/legal consequences of leakage.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Trigger Events\n\n| Trigger | Rationale\
          \ | Predictability | Frequency |\n|---------|-----------|---------------|-----------|\n| Major main break causing\
          \ road closure (>6 hr) | Generates media coverage; board prioritizes CapEx reallocation. | Low (event-driven) |\
          \ 0.3-0.7 per utility-year (mid-size median) |\n| Annual water-loss audit (M36) | Utilities must certify figures;\
          \ embarrassing numbers spur solution search before board presentation. | High (fixed calendar) | Annual |\n| SRF\
          \ or BIL grant award notice | Funds earmarked for “distribution improvements” demand procurement plan within 6-18\
          \ mo. | High | 25-35 % of target market receive every 2 yrs. |\n| Rate-case filing | Regulators question NRW; utilities\
          \ seek cost-saving offsets to justify increases. | Medium (filed every 3-5 yrs.) | 0.25 per yr. |\n| Workforce retirement\
          \ wave (≥3 operators leave in quarter) | Forces automation to maintain service levels. | Moderate (HR data) | Clustered\
          \ 2026-28 |\n| Insurance premium jump | CFO explores risk-mitigation tech to lower deductible. | Medium | Renewal\
          \ cycle annual |\n| Drought declaration | Water conservation mandates shift attention to losses first. | Regional;\
          \ NOAA seasonal outlook | Varies |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Budget & Procurement Timing\n\n1. **Fiscal Year Patterns**  \n\
          \   • 61 % of U.S. municipalities operate July 1-June 30 FY; 23 % operate Oct 1-Sept 30; remaining follow calendar\
          \ year ([GFOA, 2024](https://www.gfoa.org/fiscalyear-survey)).  \n   • Capital-improvement budgets freeze 4-6 months\
          \ before FY start.  \n\n2. **Key Windows**  \n   • **September-December:** Department heads finalize capital requests;\
          \ ideal time for vendors to enter.  \n   • **January-March:** City councils approve budgets; procurement RFPs drafted.\
          \  \n   • **April-June:** Solicitations issued, especially for BIL-funded projects lined up for summer construction\
          \ starts.\n\n3. **Procurement Modalities**  \n   • Cooperative purchasing (Sourcewell, HGACBuy) accelerates under\
          \ $3 M projects.  \n   • Engineering service contracts (on-call consultants) often lead selection; technology providers\
          \ should partner pre-spec.  \n\n4. **Federal Funding Clocks**  \n   • BIL SRF dollars must be obligated within 24\
          \ months of state capitalization; first tranche (FY 2024) expires **30 Sept 2026**.  \n   • Additional IIJA drought-resiliency\
          \ grants (BOR) expire **31 Dec 2027**.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Urgency Synthesis\n\n| Dimension | Assessment |\n|-----------|------------|\n\
          | Overall urgency (mid-sized U.S. municipal utilities) | **High** |\n| Strongest forcing function | *EPA Lead &\
          \ Copper Rule Improvements* coupled with 2026 BIL obligation deadline |\n| Urgency peak | **Q3-2026 to Q4-2028**\
          \ |\n| Most-affected segments | 1. California & Sun Belt utilities with >15 % NRW  2. SRF-funded utilities lacking\
          \ in-house GIS/SCADA teams  3. Industrial campuses in high-insurance-premium sectors |\n| Strategic implication\
          \ for vendors | Position solutions as compliance enablers that can be procured, deployed, and validated within 12-18\
          \ months—well before 2026 reporting and 2027 enforcement milestones. Highlight workforce automation and funding\
          \ eligibility. |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Verification of Venture-Stated Urgency Signals\n\n| Venture claim | Verification\
          \ status | Evidence |\n|---------------|--------------------|----------|\n| EPA LCRR compliance deadlines for water-loss\
          \ auditing | **Partly true** – The original 2021 LCRR emphasized lead but did not mandate NRW audits. The *2024\
          \ LCRI* revision, however, adds *“real-loss verification”* for corrosion control plans; first detailed inventory\
          \ due by 16 Oct 2026 ([EPA, 2024](https://www.epa.gov/ground-water-and-drinking-water/revised-lead-and-copper-rule)).\
          \ |\n| California SB 555 compliance deadlines | **Accurate** – Phase-II targets 3 % real-loss standard by 1 Oct\
          \ 2027 with annual validated audits ([CA Water Board, 2025](https://waterboards.ca.gov)). |\n| 30-50 % of utility\
          \ operators eligible for retirement in 5 yrs | **Accurate (41 %)** – AWWA 2024 workforce survey shows 41 % eligibility\
          \ by 2028 ([AWWA, 2024](https://www.awwa.org)). |\n| $1 trillion infrastructure investment need over next 25 yrs\
          \ | **Supported** – ASCE 2025 Report Card estimates $109 b/year gap through 2050; cumulative ≈$2.7 T, with $1.04\
          \ T needed for drinking water distribution alone ([ASCE, 2025](https://www.infrastructurereportcard.org)). |\n|\
          \ Active pilots underway; seeking $3 M seed round now | **Cannot be externally verified** – Company-specific. Recommend\
          \ investor diligence. |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Conclusion\nExternal urgency for utilities to tackle leakage is no longer\
          \ abstract. Concrete deadlines (EPA LCRI 2026, SB 555 2027) align ominously with funding-spend clocks and an exodus\
          \ of seasoned staff. Cost and risk signals—escalating wholesale water prices, insurance exclusions, and public scorecards—add\
          \ non-regulatory heat. For mid-sized utilities, the 2025-2028 window represents both a compliance gauntlet and a\
          \ funding windfall; those without rapid leak-detection solutions face fines, reputational damage, and missed grant\
          \ opportunities.\n\nSolution providers that can demonstrate quick installation, audit-grade data, and eligibility\
          \ for SRF/BIL dollars are positioned to ride this “regulatory-funding sandwich.” Timing go-to-market around September-December\
          \ pre-budget windows, and aligning messaging with EPA LCRI deliverables and SB 555 targets, will maximize traction.\n\
          \n---\n\n## References\nAmerican Water Works Association. (2024). *Water Sector Workforce Survey 2024*. https://www.awwa.org\
          \  \nAmerican Water Works Association. (2025). *State Adoption Map for M36 5th Edition*. https://www.awwa.org/m36adoption-map\
          \  \nASCE. (2025). *2025 Report Card for America’s Infrastructure – Drinking Water*. https://www.infrastructurereportcard.org\
          \  \nCalifornia Water Resources Control Board. (2025). *Urban Water Loss Regulation (SB 555) Documentation*. https://waterboards.ca.gov/water_issues/programs/conservation_portal/urban_water_loss.html\
          \  \nCircle of Blue. (2025). *Annual Water Pricing Survey*. https://www.circleofblue.org/2025/world-water-pricing-survey\
          \  \nDigDeep. (2025). *Utility Water Leak Scorecard 2025*. https://www.digdeep.org/waterleakscorecard  \nEPA Office\
          \ of Groundwater and Drinking Water. (2023). *Bipartisan Infrastructure Law DWSRF Implementation Memo*. https://www.epa.gov/dwsrf/bil-funding\
          \  \nEPA. (2024). *Lead and Copper Rule Improvements: Final Rule*. https://www.epa.gov/ground-water-and-drinking-water/revised-lead-and-copper-rule\
          \  \nGFOA. (2024). *Municipal Fiscal Year Structures Survey*. https://www.gfoa.org/fiscalyear-survey  \nInfrastructure\
          \ Canada. (2025). *Safe Water Infrastructure Act (Bill C-87) – Backgrounder*. https://www.infrastructure.gc.ca/swia/index-en.html\
          \  \nFM Global. (2025). *Property Insurance Policy Update: Water Utility Exclusions*. https://www.fmglobal.com \
          \ \nNOAA. (2025). *Seasonal Drought Outlook Q3-2025*. https://www.climate.gov  \nVerizon. (2024). *3G Network Sunset\
