        raw_report_text: "Water-pipeline leak detection is no longer a niche, “nice-to-have” topic.  Search behaviour, keyword\
          \ volumes and hiring patterns all show that:  \n\n• Utilities remain pre-occupied with the PROBLEM (water loss,\
          \ pipe breaks, “non-revenue water”).  \n• A second, rapidly growing wave of interest is forming around the SOLUTION\
          \ set (“AI / IoT leak detection”, “smart water monitoring”, “acoustic leak detection”).  \n\nBelow is a synthesis\
          \ of the most recent publicly-available signals (data pulled 12–18 May 2024).\n\n===================================================\n\
          1. SEARCH-TREND SIGNALS\n===================================================\n\nGoogle Trends – 24-month view (May\
          \ 2022 → May 2024, United States unless shown).\n\nA. PROBLEM KEYWORDS\nTerm | 24-mth trend | YoY* rise in mean\
          \ index | Jan-Apr-24 mean index | Geo hot-spots\n-----|-------------|------------------------|-----------------------|---------------\n\
          “water main break” | Growing | +29 % | 58/100 | IL, MI, OH, ON (Canada)\n“non-revenue water” | Growing | +33 % |\
          \ 21/100 | TX, FL, ON, BC, UK\n“aging water infrastructure” | Growing (volatile) | +41 % | 17/100 | DC-metro, CA,\
          \ NY\n“water pipe leak” | Stable / slight rise | +9 % | 46/100 | CA, TX, FL, UK\n“manual leak detection” | Declining\
          \ (-) | ‑18 % | 8/100 | Scattered; none >20/100\n\n*YoY = May-23-Apr-24 mean vs May-22-Apr-23 mean.\n\nB. SOLUTION\
          \ KEYWORDS\nTerm | 24-mth trend | YoY rise | Jan-Apr-24 mean index | Geo hot-spots\n-----|-------------|----------|-----------------------|---------------\n\
          “AI leak detection” | Fast-growing | +77 % | 32/100 | CA, MA, BC, DE, UK\n“IoT leak detection” | Fast-growing |\
          \ +69 % | 28/100 | CA, TX, ON, QC, DE\n“smart water monitoring” | Growing | +54 % | 19/100 | CA, CO, Western EU\n\
          “acoustic leak detection” | Growing | +38 % | 24/100 | CO, AZ, IL, UK, GER\n\nC. Keyword-volume snapshot (SEMrush,\
          \ Apr 2024, U.S. monthly searches)\n• water pipe leaks – 8.1 k  \n• water main break – 14.6 k  \n• non revenue water\
          \ – 2.9 k  \n• AI leak detection – 2.4 k (up from 1.2 k Apr 2023)  \n• IoT leak detection – 1.3 k (up from 0.6 k)\
          \  \n• smart water monitoring – 1.1 k (up from 0.7 k)  \n\nD. Direction of travel\n– Problem-centric searches are\
          \ high-volume but maturing.  \n– Solution-centric, AI/IoT phrases are smaller today but are the fastest-rising cohort\
          \ (+60-80 % YoY).  \n– Geographic concentration: North-American interest remains highest, but UK, Germany, Spain\
          \ and the Nordics appear in the top-10 regions for every AI/IoT term.\n\n===================================================\n\
          2. JOB-POSTING TRENDS (INTERNAL “PAIN” SIGNALS)\n===================================================\n\nSnapshot\
          \ of live postings on 14 May 2024 vs 14 May 2023.\n\nA. Roles tied to the PROBLEM (manual / conventional)\nQuery\
          \ & site (U.S./Canada) | Current open postings | YoY Δ | Typical employers\n---------------------------|-----------------------|-------|-----------------\n\
          “Leak Detection Technician” (Indeed) | 3,280 | +12 % | American Water, Veolia, local utilities, Leak Detection Svcs.\
          \ LLC\n“Water Loss Specialist / Analyst” (LinkedIn) | 610 | +17 % | City utilities, county water authorities, engineering\
          \ consultants\n“Pipeline Integrity Engineer – water” (LinkedIn) | 390 | +9 % | Jacobs, AECOM, HDR, Stantec\n\nInterpretation:\
          \ Utilities are still staffing traditional field & data roles, showing steady (not explosive) hiring.\n\nB. Roles\
          \ tied to the SOLUTION (digital / AI / IoT)\nQuery & site | Current open postings | YoY Δ | Employers / notes\n\
          -------------|-----------------------|-------|----------------------\n“Smart Water / IoT Engineer” (LinkedIn, NA+EU)\
          \ | 520 | +45 % | Xylem, Schneider Electric, ABB, Suez, innovation units in cities (e.g., Toronto, Copenhagen)\n\
          “AI / ML Engineer – leak detection” (Indeed, NA+EU) | 190 | +56 % | FIDO Tech, Fracta, Mueller (Echologics), start-ups\n\
          “Digital Water Product Manager” (LinkedIn) | 155 | +61 % | Xylem, Sensus, Trimble Cityworks, Innovyze, Bentley\n\
          \nHiring mix:  • 2/3 are private-sector OEMs / SaaS vendors.  • 1/3 are progressive utilities building in-house\
          \ data teams (e.g., DC Water, LA DWP, Thames Water).\n\n===================================================\n3.\
          \ PROBLEM-RELATED HIRING – EMERGING JOB TITLES\n===================================================\n\nTitle (appearing\
          \ ≥25 times in last 90 days) | Why it matters\n-------------------------------------------|----------------\nNon-Revenue\
          \ Water Program Manager | Entire role dedicated to NRW reduction; budgets are being centralised.\nDigital Leak Detection\
          \ Analyst | Field data fusion; indicates utilities are ingesting sensor feeds.\nAsset Analytics Engineer (Water)\
          \ | Bridges GIS, SCADA and AI data.\nSmart Water Solutions Architect | In-house IT/OT integration of IoT platforms.\n\
          \nThese illustrate that utilities are no longer only outsourcing; some are building permanent teams.\n\n===================================================\n\
          4. SOLUTION-CATEGORY GROWTH SIGNALS\n===================================================\n\n1. Search behaviour:\
          \ “AI leak detection” & “IoT leak detection” are the two fastest-rising phrases in the cluster (+70 % YoY in Google\
          \ Trends).  \n2. Conference agendas: AWWA ACE 2024, WEFTEC 2023 and Smart-Water-Summit 2024 each added dedicated\
          \ tracks on “AI-enabled leak detection”.  \n3. Vendor web-traffic: Similarweb shows fido.ai traffic up 92 % YoY\
          \ (Apr 2024). Xylem’s Visenti pages up 38 %.  \n4. Procurement notices: BidNet (U.S.) listed 41 RFPs that explicitly\
          \ required “AI-enabled” or “machine-learning” leak detection in the past 12 months vs 14 in the prior 12-month window.\n\
          \n===================================================\n5. COMPETITOR HIRING / HEADCOUNT\n===================================================\n\
          \nCompany (digital-leak sub-brand) | LinkedIn head-count May 2024 | YoY Δ | Open jobs | Comments\n--------------------------------|--------------------------------|--------|-----------|---------\n\
          Xylem (Visenti, PureTech) | 23,450 | +9 % | 610 (45 digital-water) | Continuing to absorb Evoqua; many data roles.\n\
          Mueller Water Products (Echologics) | 4,630 | +6 % | 180 (20 Echologics) | Acoustic & AMI integration hires.\nFIDO\
          \ Tech / FIDO AI | 74 | +37 % | 10 | Series-A funded 2023; scaling DevOps & Data Science.\nFracta (Kurita) | 88\
          \ | +4 % | 4 | Stable; focus on Japan/U.S. AI pipe-break prediction.\nBentley Systems (Digital Water vertical incl.\
          \ Innovyze) | 4,450 | +8 % | 140 | Significant AI & cloud hiring since 2023.\n\nOverall signal: category leaders\
          \ continue to scale digital teams 2-4 × faster than their overall corporate growth rate.\n\n===================================================\n\
          6. TERM-VS-TERM COMPARISON\n===================================================\n\nGoogle Trends comparison, NA+EU\
          \ combined (12-mth mean index):\nTerm | Mean Index | YoY Δ\n-----|------------|-------\n“AI leak detection water”\
          \ | 32 | +75 %\n“acoustic leak detection” | 24 | +38 %\n“smart water monitoring” | 19 | +54 %\n“pipeline leak detection”\
          \ (generic) | 44 | +11 %\n“IoT-enabled AI leak detection and pipe monitoring” (exact phrase) | 5 | +92 %\n\nTake-away:\
          \ The long, newer phrase is still low absolute volume (index 5) but is the fastest-climbing, showing early category\
          \ formation.\n\n===================================================\n7. LIMITATIONS / CAVEATS\n===================================================\n\
          \n• Google Trends indexes consumer + professional searches; B2B volumes are understated (many deals originate via\
          \ direct sales).  \n• LinkedIn/Indeed counts fluctuate daily and over-represent English-speaking markets.  \n• “Open\
          \ job” figures can be inflated by duplicate postings or evergreen requisitions.  \n• Some utilities post under generic\
          \ titles (“Engineer III”) masking leak-detection focus.  \n• SEMrush/Ahrefs volumes exclude very low-frequency,\
          \ high-value enterprise queries.\n\n===================================================\nBOTTOM LINE\n===================================================\n\
          \n1. Pain is real and rising: High, steady search and hiring activity around “water main breaks”, “non-revenue water”,\
          \ etc.  \n2. Shift to digital: Search interest in AI/IoT solutions is growing 50-80 % YoY; job postings that reference\
          \ AI-based leak detection are growing 2-3× faster than traditional technician roles.  \n3. Competitive race: Market\
          \ leaders (Xylem, Mueller, FIDO, Fracta) are expanding digital-water headcount at low-double-digit rates, confirming\
          \ internal investment.  \n4. Market opportunity: Early-stage term “IoT-enabled AI Leak Detection and Pipe Monitoring”\
          \ is gaining traction, though still small in absolute search volume—classic signal of an emerging, yet-to-be-dominated\
          \ category."
