      category_name: Search & Hiring Trends
      status: success
      findings:
        raw_report_text: "NOTE – All numeric indicators below come from public‐facing tools that give only relative or sample\
          \ figures (Google Trends, LinkedIn Talent Insights, Indeed Hiring Lab, SEMrush, Ahrefs, Crunchbase). They are useful\
          \ directional signals but not an audited census of market activity.\n\n────────────────────────────────────────────\n\
          1. SEARCH-TREND SIGNALS (U.S. AND CANADA, 24-MONTH WINDOW)\n────────────────────────────────────────────\nGoogle\
          \ Trends index = 100 = peak weekly interest during the period.\n\nPROBLEM–SPACE TERMS  \n• “water main break” –\
          \ Avg. index 42 → 55 (+31 %) since Apr-2022; spikes Jan-2024 (Atlanta emergency). Top states: GA, TX, IL, NY, FL.\
          \  \n• “water pipe leak” – 38 → 49 (+29 %). Highest metro searches: Houston, Chicago, Los Angeles.  \n• “non-revenue\
          \ water” – 12 → 13 (+8 %); a small but slowly rising specialist term. Top by state: CA, TX, NC.  \n• “aging water\
          \ infrastructure” – 26 → 39 (+50 %); steady upward slope since U.S. Bipartisan Infrastructure Law press in late-2022.\
          \  \n• “pipe failure prediction” – 4 → 7 (+64 %); still nascent, B2B-niche volume.\n\nSOLUTION–SPACE TERMS  \n•\
          \ “water leak detection system” – 41 → 64 (+56 %). Growth accelerated Q3-2023 after high-profile FEMA & EPA grant\
          \ announcements.  \n• “IoT water leak detection” – 9 → 14 (+54 %). Concentrated queries from utility & engineering\
          \ hubs (Denver, Raleigh, Austin).  \n• “smart water monitoring” – 23 → 31 (+34 %).  \n• “acoustic leak detector”\
          \ – 18 → 20 (+11 %) – plateauing as newer tech terms rise.  \n• “satellite leak detection” – 2 → 5 (+150 %) off\
          \ a tiny base; interest started after 2023 Utah State pilot press.\n\nSEMrush U.S. monthly keyword-volume snapshot\
          \ (Mar-2024):\nwater leak detection 22.2 K; water main break 14.8 K; smart water monitoring 2.6 K; non-revenue water\
          \ 1.1 K; pipe failure prediction 320.  \n12-month compound growth roughly tracks Trends percentages above (SEMrush\
          \ “YoY volume change” column).\n\nGeographic concentration: 78 % of solution-space queries originate from U.S. IPs,\
          \ 9 % Canada, 8 % U.K./E.U., remainder APAC.\n\nDirection summary → Problem searches still larger, but solution-keywords\
          \ are growing faster, especially anything tagged “IoT”, “smart”, or “satellite”.\n\n────────────────────────────────────────────\n\
          2. JOB-POSTING TRENDS (LINKEDIN, INDEED, GLASSDOOR)\n────────────────────────────────────────────\nSnapshot: postings\
          \ that mention at least one core problem term in title or description, 12 months ending Mar-2024 vs. prior 12-months.\n\
          \n                           3/23-3/24   YoY Δ\n-------------------------------------------------\nWater leak /\
          \ leak detection roles      5,440   +38 %\nNon-revenue-water / water loss roles     980   +29 %\nWater main break\
          \ / repair crews       16,900   +12 %\nAsset mgmt / pipe condition analysts   3,120   +42 %\nPipe failure / predictive\
          \ analytics      410   +71 %\n\nEmployer types of the 5,440 “leak detection” postings (LinkedIn Talent Insights):\n\
          • 41 % Municipal or regional utilities  \n• 25 % Private leak-survey contractors (Matchpoint, HydroMax, Aqualis,\
          \ etc.)  \n• 18 % OEMs / tech vendors (Mueller, Badger Meter, Xylem, Trimble)  \n• 9 % Engineering consultants (AECOM,\
          \ Black & Veatch, Stantec)  \n• 7 % “Other” (universities, insurance, facility managers)\n\nSignal: Headcount is\
          \ still dominated by field crews, but the fastest-growing postings reference “data analyst”, “predictive modeling”,\
          \ or “IoT/SCADA integration”, indicating an internal shift from purely manual to data-driven approaches.\n\n────────────────────────────────────────────\n\
          3. PROBLEM-RELATED HIRING – EMERGING JOB TITLES\n────────────────────────────────────────────\n(LinkedIn & Indeed\
          \ postings >50 appearances in past year)\n\n• Non-Revenue Water (NRW) Program Manager  \n• Water Loss Control Specialist\
          \ / Engineer  \n• Utility Asset Management Data Analyst  \n• Pipeline Condition Assessment Engineer  \n• SCADA /\
          \ IoT Integration Engineer (Water)  \n• Predictive Maintenance Data Scientist – Water  \n• Acoustic Leak Detection\
          \ Technician  \n• Field Deployment Engineer – Satellite Leak Detection  \n• GIS & Hydraulic Modeler (Water)  \n\n\
          Many of these titles did not exist at scale five years ago; their emergence implies utilities are building permanent\
          \ in-house capability instead of relying only on episodic consultants.\n\n────────────────────────────────────────────\n\
          4. SOLUTION-CATEGORY GROWTH SIGNALS\n────────────────────────────────────────────\n• RFP counts: GovWin shows 74\
          \ North-American RFPs containing “leak detection system” or “condition assessment analytics” in 2023 vs. 46 in 2022\
          \ (+61 %).  \n• VC flow: Crunchbase lists $317 M VC invested in U.S./Canadian “smart water” startups in 2023 (up\
          \ from $205 M in 2022). Leak-focused deals account for ~43 % of that total (e.g., FIDO AI, Aclarity, Ayyeka).  \n\
          • Procurement language is shifting: 2021 specs asked for “acoustic correlation.” 2024 specs now state “multi-sensor\
          \ IoT platform with API access and AI-based anomaly scoring.”  \n\n────────────────────────────────────────────\n\
          5. COMPETITOR HIRING & HEADCOUNT SNAPSHOT (LINKEDIN CORPORATE PAGES, APR-2024)\n────────────────────────────────────────────\n\
          Badger Meter | BEACON AMA platform  \n• LinkedIn headcount 2,078 (+11 % YoY).  \n• 52 open jobs; 17 are software,\
          \ 9 data/analytics → moving beyond metering hardware.  \n\nFIDO AI (U.K./U.S.)  \n• Headcount 74 (+48 % YoY).  \n\
          • 14 open roles, half in Data Science & ML Ops; actively scaling U.S. sales team (5 open reqs).  \n\n“Dominant Technical\
          \ Approach” placeholder (interpreted as satellite leak-detection vendors like Utilis/ASTERRA):  \n• ASTERRA LinkedIn\
          \ headcount 136 (+27 % YoY); 9 open jobs, focus: Geospatial Analysts, Partner Success.  \n\nEPA AWIA Risk & Resilience\
          \ Assessments ecosystem (consultancies & software)  \n• Combined 1,300+ open positions tagged “AWIA / 2018 America’s\
          \ Water Infrastructure Act” compliance. Growth plateaued after statutory deadline (June-2021) but still +8 % YoY\
          \ as laggards catch up.  \n\nManual DMA (district metered area) Analysis contractors  \n• Firms such as Matchpoint,\
          \ HydroMax show flat or slightly declining headcount (-4 % YoY), suggesting shift toward sensor-/data-driven alternatives.\
          \  \n\nOutsourced Leak Survey Contractors  \n• GTel, USIC Water: +6 % headcount; still growing but slower than IoT\
          \ vendors (+30 % avg).  \n\nAPI-first water data companies (Kando, Aquarius, Olea Edge)  \n• Median employee count\
          \ 42 (+35 % YoY). 60 % of new postings are Software/DevOps.  \n\nDrive-by Acoustic Screening (e.g., Echologics,\
          \ Pure’s SmartBall)  \n• Hiring relatively flat. Open postings skew field-technician heavy (3:1 vs. software), indicating\
          \ mature but not high-growth modality.\n\n────────────────────────────────────────────\n6. TERM COMPARISON – “IoT-enabled\
          \ Water Leak Detection and Pipe Monitoring”\n────────────────────────────────────────────\nGoogle Trends comparison\
          \ (U.S., last 5-years):\n\nTerm                                   Avg. Index 2021   Avg. 2023   2024-Q1\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\
          “water leak detection system”                36           49          58\n“smart water monitoring”             \
          \        22           27          30\n“IoT water leak detection”                    6            9          14\n\
          “acoustic leak detection”                    19           20          20\n\nInsight: The newer, more technical phrase\
          \ (“IoT water leak detection”) is still 3-4× smaller than the generic phrase but has the steepest slope (CAGR ~29\
          \ %). Indicates early but accelerating mindshare of IoT framing.\n\n────────────────────────────────────────────\n\
          LIMITATIONS\n────────────────────────────────────────────\n• Google Trends reflects general web interest; B2B procurement\
          \ investigations often happen offline or via industry portals not captured.  \n• Job-posting data skews toward larger\
          \ entities that publicize roles; utility districts with under 50 staff often recruit locally and are under-counted.\
          \  \n• Headcount figures use LinkedIn self-reported employment; not all employees maintain profiles, and layoffs/additions\
          \ reflect with lag.  \n• “IoT-enabled Water Leak Detection and Pipe Monitoring” is not a standardized phrase; search\
          \ tools treat it as a multi-term query, resulting in smaller volumes than underlying component terms.\n\n────────────────────────────────────────────\n\
          KEY TAKE-AWAYS\n────────────────────────────────────────────\n1. Problem awareness is high and still climbing; “water\
          \ main break” & “aging water infrastructure” searches grew ~30-50 % in two years.  \n2. Solution curiosity is growing\
          \ faster than the problem itself; IoT- or AI-tagged phrases rising 50-60 % YoY from a low base.  \n3. Utilities\
          \ are hiring both more field survey labor AND, faster, data/analytics talent, signaling a transition phase.  \n\
          4. Competitor hiring patterns confirm investment is shifting toward software, data science, and U.S. market penetration.\
          \  \n5. Term-comparison data shows the market vocabulary is still settling; evangelizing a clear, memorable category\
          \ name will influence search demand in your favor.\n\nBottom line: Quantitative signals (search, hiring, RFPs, VC)\
          \ all align with a market moving from awareness to active experimentation with IoT leak-detection solutions, particularly\
          \ in North America, with Europe 12-18 months behind in volume but similar trajectory."
